\end_deeper 
\layout Enumerate

Show that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\langle n^{2}\right\rangle =\lambda te^{-\lambda t}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{n(\lambda t)^{(n-1)}}{(n-1)!},\end{equation}

\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\langle n^{2}\right\rangle $
\end_inset 

 represents the expectation of 
\begin_inset Formula $n^{2}$
\end_inset 

 under a Poisson distribution.
 
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard

Then by making the substitution 
\begin_inset Formula $m=n-1$
\end_inset 

 show that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\left\langle n^{2}\right\rangle =(\lambda t)^{2}+\lambda t.\end{equation}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

What, therefore, is the variance of a Poisson distribution? 
\layout Standard


\emph on 
Hint: There are similarities with the proof of the mean under a Poisson
 distribution we went through in the lecture.
\end_deeper 
